funcrunch <web-goog...@funcrunch.org> wrote:
>
> The point of the game is to win, i.e., ascend.

False.

The general goal of all games - enjoyment.  The general
goal of all businesses - profit.  The general goal of all
competitions - win.

As such the goal during the tourney month is to win.

While ascending is fun, I wouldn't still be playing after
well over a decade if the play itself wasn't fun enough to
be the goal all by itself.

> We can debate endlessly
> about how the scoring is broken, Gehennom is boring, yada yada, but
> the goal of the game of Nethack remains: offer the amulet to your god/
> dess at the high altar, and attain demigod/dess status.

That's the stated the character's goal, but the character's
goal isn't the goal of the game itself.  And since I control
the character it doesn't have to be my goal.

Do I quit a game because I reach Gehennom?  No.  I
continue on unless I ascend or die.

> Therefore, a
> strategy such as the protection racket to lower your AC as quickly and
> cheaply as possible, would naturally have as its goal making ascension
> easier, or more assured. Unless I hear that a sizable number of
> players who are actually winning the game are using the protection
> racket as a primary tool for doing so, I can't believe that it's that
> useful of a strategy.

It's a valid strategy among many.  The fact that high ascension
percentage players tend to not use it is instructive - I view it as
a learning method.  For my trying the protection racket is a way
to learn better survivability to make it to the mid-game.  Should I
ever get in excess of 50% of my character to the mid-game with
or without the protection rack, I will have learned what it has to
offer as a learning mechanism.



